{"answers":[],"asked":[{"category":"errors","id":"Q-6","impact":3,"impactReason":"The choice changes how existing database schema and data are handled.","options":["Apply a reviewed database migration during deployment, preserving existing data; use EnsureCreated only for genuinely empty databases.","Require a fresh, empty database for this release and do not migrate existing databases.","Apply EF Core migrations automatically at application startup."],"reason":"This supports the new persisted records without treating an existing database as if it were empty.","recommended":"Apply a reviewed database migration during deployment, preserving existing data; use EnsureCreated only for genuinely empty databases.","score":9,"spans":[],"text":"How should schema changes be applied to databases that already exist when the new persistent tables are introduced?","uncertainty":3},{"category":"errors","id":"Q-7","impact":3,"impactReason":"The choice determines how the first authorized account is created and who can create it.","options":["Have deployment provision the initial account outside the API; do not add an API bootstrap flow.","Add a one-time administrative bootstrap operation for creating the first account.","Allow the demo seeder to create the initial account even when Seed:Demo is false."],"reason":"This avoids adding an unrequested account-creation path while keeping demo seeding disabled.","recommended":"Have deployment provision the initial account outside the API; do not add an API bootstrap flow.","score":9,"spans":[],"text":"With demo seeding disabled, how should the first PostgreSQL account be made available so the API can authorize callers?","uncertainty":3},{"category":"errors","id":"Q-8","impact":3,"impactReason":"The choice determines how staff tokens are trusted and who can access staff operations.","options":["Identify the organization's existing staff provider and supply its authorization URL and validation settings; do not introduce a new provider.","Select and integrate a new staff identity provider for this API.","Do not validate staff identity-provider tokens in this release."],"reason":"This follows the stated use of an existing provider without inventing a new identity system.","recommended":"Identify the organization's existing staff provider and supply its authorization URL and validation settings; do not introduce a new provider.","score":9,"spans":[],"text":"Which staff identity provider and concrete authorization-URL and token-validation settings should the API use?","uncertainty":3},{"category":"errors","id":"Q-9","impact":2,"impactReason":"The choice affects whether tests and factory checks start and which routes they can exercise.","options":["Provide valid nonproduction settings for the chosen provider through test and factory configuration.","Skip identity-provider configuration validation for test and factory runs.","Use production identity-provider settings for the test and factory runs."],"reason":"This meets the startup-validation requirement while keeping test runs on nonproduction settings.","recommended":"Provide valid nonproduction settings for the chosen provider through test and factory configuration.","score":6,"spans":[],"text":"How should the test and factory runs obtain the valid nonproduction identity-provider settings required for startup?","uncertainty":3},{"category":"errors","id":"Q-10","impact":3,"impactReason":"Authentication and credential-storage choices affect account access and sensitive data.","options":["Keep behavior limited to the locked contract; use standard secure password hashing for stored passwords, and do not add new lockout or one-time-code policies.","Specify explicit responses and rules for wrong passwords, unknown emails, wrong, expired or reused codes, and locked accounts, plus password-storage requirements.","Delegate customer authentication to an external identity provider instead of implementing the contract's email-and-password and one-time-code flow."],"reason":"This avoids adding unrequested authentication policies while not storing passwords insecurely.","recommended":"Keep behavior limited to the locked contract; use standard secure password hashing for stored passwords, and do not add new lockout or one-time-code policies.","score":9,"spans":[],"text":"How much customer-authentication behavior beyond the locked contract should be specified for failed logins and password storage?","uncertainty":3},{"category":"errors","id":"Q-11","impact":3,"impactReason":"The choice determines which staff identities map to permissions and whether unmatched or disabled accounts can access data.","options":["Match the token's email claim to the account email, and deny access when no matching enabled account exists.","Match a stable subject identifier (`sub`) to a stored account identifier, and deny access when no matching enabled account exists.","Treat every valid staff token as authorized even if no enabled PostgreSQL account matches."],"reason":"Email matching is the smallest mapping consistent with the specified account-based authorization; unmatched or disabled accounts remain unauthorized.","recommended":"Match the token's email claim to the account email, and deny access when no matching enabled account exists.","score":9,"spans":[],"text":"Which token claim should identify the PostgreSQL staff account, and what should happen if a valid token has no matching or has a disabled account?","uncertainty":3}],"checks":{"critic":[{"finding":"REQ-4 and AC-4.1 rely on ASM-1 (\"between two and five rows\"), but the accepted assumptions list is empty, so this numeric bound was never accepted and has no source in the intent or answers.","reqId":"REQ-4","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-4 seeds only the empty tables and requires every reference to resolve, but AC-4.2 never says how a seeded child row in an empty table should point at a parent table that already holds non-demo rows with unknown ids, and it never checks that the empty tables were actually seeded.","reqId":"REQ-4","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-4.1's \"the rows are based on contract examples\" and the undefined term \"contract-backed table\" give no concrete, checkable condition.","reqId":"REQ-4","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-3.1 only checks that `GET /` returns 200 with Seed:Demo true, so it never observes that SeedData.Run ran or that any sample rows exist.","reqId":"REQ-3","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"The spec adds persistent entities but keeps startup on `EnsureCreated` (C-5), which does nothing once tables exist, and it has no migration or schema-evolution plan for existing databases despite the 'migration' risk tag.","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":6,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-2.3 covers only the before-packing success case of changeCustomerOrder; there is no AC for changing an order that is already packed (the contract declares 409), for a non-owned order, or for a missing order (404).","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"Receipts are created Quarantined (AC-2.2), but the spec defines no allowed state transitions out of Quarantined and no rejected ones, even though it says to apply the lifecycle constraints described in the contract.","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"With Seed:Demo off and an empty database, no account exists, yet REQ-9 authorizes every caller from a PostgreSQL account record; the spec has no way to bootstrap the first account, and AC-5.1 assumes 'an account row provisioned by deployment' with no anchor for that process.","reqId":"REQ-5","rubric":6,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"REQ-7 has no anchors and refers to 'the existing staff identity provider', but current-behaviour shows no identity-provider integration or configuration anywhere in the codebase.","reqId":"REQ-7","rubric":5,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-7.1 says the URL comes back in a response field named `message`, and AC-7.3 names no field at all; neither is anchored to the contract's response schema for getStaffAuthorizationUrl.","reqId":"REQ-7","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Making startup fail on missing IdP settings will break the existing HealthTests.ApiIsUp and the factory runs, which supply no such settings today; REQ-7/REQ-8 claim these checks 'use valid nonproduction settings' without anchors and quietly require changing test and factory configuration.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":7,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-8.3 expects 'process exit status is 0' from a host that is still serving requests, and AC-8.2 expects a process exit status from an in-process test host; neither outcome can be observed.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 have essentially the same given/when/then, so the acceptance criteria are duplicated.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":1,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-1.3 expects `GET /swagger` to return 200, but Swagger UI middleware normally redirects /swagger to /swagger/index.html, and no anchor supports a 200.","reqId":"REQ-1","rubric":5,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-1.2 says an out-of-scope filter returns only in-scope data (a filtered 200), but AC-9.1 says the same situation (filter warehouseId=20 outside the caller's scope) returns 403.","reqId":"REQ-1","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-9.1 uses a `warehouseId` query parameter on 'the stock collection', but nothing in the anchored contract excerpts shows that parameter or a stock operation (the anchored listReceipts takes only Search, Status, DateFrom and DateTo).","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-9 has no error paths for customer authentication: wrong password, unknown email, wrong, expired or reused one-time code, and locked accounts are all missing; it also doesn't say how passwords are stored, despite the auth and pii risk tags.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"REQ-9 doesn't say how a staff IdP token maps to a PostgreSQL account record (which claim is matched), or what happens when a token is valid but has no account record, or a disabled one.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-9.2 assumes 'a valid one-time code available for that account', but the spec never says how the code is delivered or how a test reads it through a public surface.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"}],"lint":[{"blocking":true,"check":"L1 ids","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L2 req-ac","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L3 ears","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L4 vague","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L5 nfr","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":false,"check":"L6 scope","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L8 anchors","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":false,"check":"L9 size","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L10 trace","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true},{"blocking":true,"check":"L11 observable","details":"ok","fails":[],"hint":"Each Then names what a test reads, using at least one of these words: response, status, returned, row, record, call, request, sent, email, message, error, event, field, value, count, list, file, screen, page, shown, displayed. \"Sign-in succeeds\" fails; \"The response status is 200\" passes.","passed":true},{"blocking":false,"check":"L12 literals","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true}],"roundTrip":{"droppedSpans":[],"inventedCapabilities":[]}},"problemOf":{"Q-10":[6],"Q-11":[7],"Q-6":[1],"Q-7":[3],"Q-8":[4],"Q-9":[5]},"problems":[{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-2","text":"The spec adds persistent entities but keeps startup on `EnsureCreated` (C-5), which does nothing once tables exist, and it has no migration or schema-evolution plan for existing databases despite the 'migration' risk tag."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-2","text":"AC-2.3 covers only the before-packing success case of changeCustomerOrder; there is no AC for changing an order that is already packed (the contract declares 409), for a non-owned order, or for a missing order (404)."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-5","text":"With Seed:Demo off and an empty database, no account exists, yet REQ-9 authorizes every caller from a PostgreSQL account record; the spec has no way to bootstrap the first account, and AC-5.1 assumes 'an account row provisioned by deployment' with no anchor for that process."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-7","text":"REQ-7 has no anchors and refers to 'the existing staff identity provider', but current-behaviour shows no identity-provider integration or configuration anywhere in the codebase."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-8","text":"Making startup fail on missing IdP settings will break the existing HealthTests.ApiIsUp and the factory runs, which supply no such settings today; REQ-7/REQ-8 claim these checks 'use valid nonproduction settings' without anchors and quietly require changing test and factory configuration."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-9","text":"REQ-9 has no error paths for customer authentication: wrong password, unknown email, wrong, expired or reused one-time code, and locked accounts are all missing; it also doesn't say how passwords are stored, despite the auth and pii risk tags."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-9","text":"REQ-9 doesn't say how a staff IdP token maps to a PostgreSQL account record (which claim is matched), or what happens when a token is valid but has no account record, or a disabled one."}],"round":1,"settled":[],"spec":{"assumptions":["ASM-1: For this specification, “a few” sample rows means between two and five rows per empty contract-backed table."],"nfrs":[],"outOfScope":["Portal screens and web-app implementation (I-5, Q-1).","Product-story behavior that is not defined by the locked OpenAPI contract (Q-1)."],"requirements":[{"acceptance":[{"given":"Every operation and response case defined in `contracts/openapi.yaml`, including contract-valid and contract-invalid requests and the declared authentication and authorization cases.","id":"AC-1.1","level":"api","then":"The response status, content type, response fields, and error body conform to that operation's OpenAPI request and response definitions.","when":"A test client sends the corresponding HTTP requests to every declared operation."},{"given":"A caller authenticated as an account with assigned warehouse or organization scopes calls a collection or record operation using contract-defined filters.","id":"AC-1.2","level":"api","then":"The response status and returned list or record contain only data within the caller's assigned scope and reflect the supplied filters.","when":"The caller sends the HTTP request to that operation."},{"given":"The API is running with valid startup configuration.","id":"AC-1.3","level":"api","then":"The response status for each request is 200.","when":"A client requests `GET /`, `GET /openapi/v1.json`, and `GET /swagger`."}],"anchors":[{"lineEnd":12,"lineStart":1,"path":"contracts/openapi.yaml","quote":"openapi: 3.0.3\ninfo:\n  title: Meridian Cold Chain API\n  version: 1.0.0\n  description: \"Shared contract for the Meridian responsive web portal.\"\nservers:\n  - url: /api\npaths:\n  /auth/staff/authorization-url:\n    get:\n      operationId: getStaffAuthorizationUrl\n      summary: \"Get the configured staff identity-provider authorization URL.\""},{"lineEnd":31,"lineStart":28,"path":"App.Api/Program.cs","quote":"app.MapGet(\"/\", () => \"API is up\").ExcludeFromDescription();\n// The API's own document (/openapi/v1.json) and a page to read and try it (/swagger). Keep both: the factory's product page links to the page.\napp.MapOpenApi();\napp.UseSwaggerUI(o => o.SwaggerEndpoint(\"/openapi/v1.json\", \"API\"));"}],"ears":"The Meridian API shall implement only the operations and behavior defined i